namespace gismo {

/**
\page rh_refinement_example rh_refinement_example.cpp: moving meshes and geometry composition

This example uses \c gsAdaptiveMultiPatchBuilder to redistribute a spline mesh
according to an analytic density. It then approximates the composition of the
initial geometry and the moving parameter map, and measures the approximation
errors. It supports two-dimensional parameter domains, including surfaces in
three-dimensional space, and three-dimensional volume domains.

For a PDE-driven rh-adaptive loop, see \ref rh_poisson_example. Here the density
is prescribed analytically; the example does not solve a PDE.

\section rh_refinement_input Geometry and density

The geometry is read from the XML file selected by \c -d / \c --file, using the
\c MultiPatch ID selected by \c -a / \c --id_mp. The default is the four-patch
annulus in \c filedata/pde/annulus2d_bvp.xml, with multipatch ID 4.

The scalar density expression is always read from
\c filedata/pde/circle.xml, using \c -b / \c --id_rho (default: 1942).
Changing the geometry file does not change the density file. The expression
must accept physical coordinates of the geometry: two coordinates for a planar
domain, or three for a surface in space or a volume. For example, density 1954
is a function of \f$(x,y,z)\f$ and can be used with the cylinder surface.

Input knot intervals need not be \f$[0,1]\f$. For the original patch
\f$F_p^{\mathrm{xml}}\f$ with parameter box \f$\prod_j[a_{pj},b_{pj}]\f$,
the builder stores the affine conversion
\f$A_p(\eta)_j=a_{pj}+(b_{pj}-a_{pj})\eta_j\f$ in
\c parameterConversion(). It normalizes a copy of every knot vector, including
all stored hierarchical levels, while retaining control coefficients and NURBS
weights. The input geometry and XML are unchanged.

Writing \f$F_p=F_p^{\mathrm{xml}}\circ A_p\f$ for this exact normalized
geometry, available through \c normalizedGeometry(), the builder first projects
\f$f\circ F_p\f$ into its moving mesh spline space. For multiple
patches this projection couples matching interfaces continuously. The moving
mesh solver scales the projected monitor using \c --IntensityMAE and constructs
a parameter map
\f[
 T_p:\hat\Omega\longrightarrow\hat\Omega,
 \qquad \hat\Omega=(0,1)^d,\quad d\in\{2,3\}.
\f]
The map \f$T\f$ is stored in \c MAE.MAmapping. The physical composition is
\f$X_p=F_p\circ T_p\f$; it remains a surface when \f$d=2\f$ and
\f$F_p\f$ has three physical coordinates.
The example uses \c normalizedGeometry() for direct composition, error
evaluation, and integration, so all reference derivatives include the affine
conversion. Composition and moving mesh bases are defined on unit boxes;
marked-element bases passed to \c buildDensity must use the same coordinates.

\section rh_refinement_mapping Moving mesh and boundary treatment

The \c --uniformRefineMAE option refines the moving mesh and composition bases
before constructing the map. Degree reduction or elevation of the moving mesh
basis is controlled by \c --degreeRedution and \c --degreeMAEElevation,
respectively; a positive reduction takes precedence over elevation.

Multiple patches use the weighted elliptic MMPDE solver. The \c --exactGeo
switch also selects this solver for a single patch and fixes exterior
parameter traces at the identity. In two dimensions each sweep updates edges
and then patch interiors; in three dimensions it updates edges, faces, and
then volume interiors. Shared traces are synchronized with their interface
orientations. With \c GISMO_WITH_OPENMP enabled, patch interiors can run
concurrently after those traces have been synchronized; \c OMP_NUM_THREADS
controls the thread limit. A single patch without \c --exactGeo uses the
Monge-Ampere Picard branch.

\section rh_refinement_composition Approximating the composition

By default, \c buildColCompMultiPatch evaluates \f$F_p\circ T_p\f$ at the
composition basis anchors and interpolates those values. Let \f$X_{h,p}\f$
denote the resulting spline approximation. With \c --exactGeo, collocation
also restores the original exterior boundary control coefficients when the
refined initial geometry matches the composition basis. This correction
requires preserving the initial geometry degree.

The \c --H1 switch instead calls \c buildH1CompMultiPatch. It minimizes
\f[
 \sum_p\int_{\hat\Omega}
   \|X_{h,p}-F_p\circ T_p\|^2
   +\alpha\|D_\xi X_{h,p}-D_\xi(F_p\circ T_p)\|_F^2
   \,d\xi,
 \qquad \alpha=\texttt{gradientWeight}.
\f]
The derivatives are taken in reference coordinates, with
\f$D_\xi(F_p\circ T_p)=DF_p(T_p)\,DT_p\f$. The projection retains the
collocated exterior traces and couples matching patch interfaces continuously.
The weight \c -w / \c --gradientWeight must be finite and non-negative;
zero gives a value-only projection with the same prescribed boundary traces.
The quadrature factor \c -q / \c --quadValue must be positive.

\remark For surfaces in three-dimensional space, direct evaluation of
\f$F_p\circ T_p\f$ using \c gsComposedGeometry preserves the original surface
exactly, provided \f$T_p\f$ is a bijection of the reference domain onto itself.
Collocation and H1 fitting can introduce geometric error in the surface
interior; \c --exactGeo preserves compatible exterior boundary traces only.
For exact surface preservation, refine the analysis basis and use the direct
composition as the geometry for assembly.

The moving map is computed once. Later uniform refinement of
\c MAE.mapping_basis improves the composition approximation without
recomputing \f$T\f$. By default only the final level selected by
\c --uniformRefine is measured. Since \c last starts as \c true and switches
toggle their initial value, passing \c --last enables reporting all levels
from zero through \c --uniformRefine.

\section rh_refinement_errors Error measures

For each composition level, the example reports:

- \c PositionInf: the sampled maximum of
  \f$\|X_{h,p}-F_p\circ T_p\|_2\f$ over all patches.
- \c GradientInf: the sampled maximum of
  \f$\|D_\xi X_{h,p}-DF_p(T_p)DT_p\|_F\f$.
- \c Boundary: the sampled position error on exterior patch sides only;
  internal interfaces are excluded.
- \c JacInt: the absolute value of the integral of the difference between
  the approximate and direct composition Jacobian determinants, as evaluated
  by the expression assembler. This is an integral discrepancy, not an
  integral of the absolute pointwise error.

Position, gradient, and boundary errors use a separate reference sampling grid
with sampling factor 200; they are sampled maxima rather than certified
supremum norms. When multiple uniform levels are reported, the table also
prints rates \f$\log_2(E_{j-1}/E_j)\f$. Its \c DoFs column counts the scalar
composition basis functions across patches, not the physical coordinate
coefficients or unknowns of a PDE.

\section rh_refinement_options Main options

<table>
<tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td>\c -d / \c --file</td><td>\c pde/annulus2d_bvp.xml</td><td>Geometry XML file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -a / \c --id_mp</td><td>4</td><td>Multipatch ID in the geometry file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -b / \c --id_rho</td><td>1942</td><td>Density expression ID in \c pde/circle.xml.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -m / \c --uniformRefineMAE</td><td>4</td><td>Initial uniform refinement steps for the mapping and composition bases.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -i / \c --iter</td><td>100</td><td>Maximum Picard sweeps for mesh redistribution.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -f / \c --IntensityMAE</td><td>9</td><td>Monitor contrast.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -r / \c --degreeRedution</td><td>0</td><td>Moving mesh degree-reduction steps, retaining degree at least one.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -s / \c --degreeMAEElevation</td><td>0</td><td>Moving mesh degree-elevation steps when reduction is zero.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -u / \c --uniformRefine</td><td>0</td><td>Additional uniform refinement steps for composition only.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c --last</td><td>last = true</td><td>Toggle final-level-only reporting off to report all uniform levels.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c --exactGeo</td><td>false</td><td>Fix exterior parameter traces and restore compatible composition boundary coefficients.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c --H1</td><td>false</td><td>Use value and reference-gradient projection instead of collocation.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -w / \c --gradientWeight</td><td>1</td><td>Gradient weight in H1 composition.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -q / \c --quadValue</td><td>2</td><td>Quadrature factor for error integration and H1 composition.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c --plot</td><td>false</td><td>Run local h-refinement and export an additional physical geometry collection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>\c -l / \c --numLRefine</td><td>1</td><td>Local h-refinement steps in the optional plotting stage.</td></tr>
</table>

The legacy options \c --degreeElevation, \c --elevDegree, and \c --nrb are
parsed but do not change the current computation. The internal
\c quRule option is not applied; this example uses Gauss-Legendre rules.

\section rh_refinement_run Building and running

From the repository root:
\code{.sh}
cmake -S . -B build -DGISMO_BUILD_EXAMPLES=ON
cmake --build build --target rh_refinement_example -j 2
cd build
\endcode

Run the default annulus, or the egg surface with a three-coordinate
density:
\code{.sh}
./bin/rh_refinement_example
./bin/rh_refinement_example -d surfaces/egg.xml -a 0 -b 1956 --exactGeo --plot
./bin/rh_refinement_example -d pde/surfacepoisson_sphere_bvp.xml -a 0 -b 1958
\endcode

Compare collocation and H1 composition on the cylinder using a fixed exterior
boundary:
\code{.sh}
./bin/rh_refinement_example -d surfaces/cylinder.xml -a 1 -b 1954 --exactGeo
./bin/rh_refinement_example -d surfaces/cylinder.xml -a 1 -b 1954 --exactGeo --H1 -w 1
\endcode

To report three composition levels while keeping the moving map unchanged:
\code{.sh}
./bin/rh_refinement_example -m 2 -i 20 -u 2 --last
\endcode

\section rh_refinement_output Output and optional local refinement

Even without \c --plot, the example writes these ParaView outputs relative to
the working directory:

- \c ParaviewOutput/density.pvd: all patch densities as separate fields on one
  reference patch.
- \c ParaviewOutput/MAE_mapping_patchN: each moving parameter map and its
  element mesh.
- \c ParaviewOutput/composition_direct.pvd and
  \c ParaviewOutput/composition_approx.pvd: the directly evaluated composition
  and its spline approximation, including Jacobians, position error, gradient
  error, and element meshes.

The composition collections are rewritten at each reported level, so their
final contents correspond to the last level. Error vectors are appended to
\c ../build/errorGeometry_analysis.txt; running from the build directory as
above places this file in that directory.

With \c --plot, the final approximate physical geometry is converted to a
hierarchical representation where necessary. The example integrates the
analytic density over each parameter element, marks cells using PUCA with
parameter 0.7, and refines with one ring of neighboring cells for
\c --numLRefine steps. This stage refines the representation of the computed
geometry; it does not recompute the moving map or repeat the composition error
analysis. The result is written to \c ParaviewOutput/solution.pvd with density
and Jacobian fields, and the example requests opening it in the local viewer.

\section rh_refinement_source Source

\include rh_refinement_example.cpp

*/

}
